Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
> [...]Killing things in the right place at the
> right time is part of the game; removing it serves no purpose.

I just want to chime in here about this statement, and the attitude
behind it.

Sometimes I get the sense that a lot of YANIs are blocked from
consideration because they would remove aspects from the game.  Like,
as here, killing things in the right place, or YANIs that would make
given conducts easier or harder to accomplish.

The perception of what is and isn't part of the game is largely
arbitrary.  Right now pudding farming IS part of the game, as is
killing Pestilence over and over via macro for billions of points, yet
a good case could be made that they shouldn't be.  That's not say that
inventing those strategies, or implementing them, isn't creative or
inventive, because they're certainly great (in the MIT sense) hacks.
But (IMHO of course) they upset some assumptions about the game that
are more important that the strategies themselves.

Other tactics, too, that currently work and require some laudatory
ingenuity, like credit cloning and price IDing, are also perhaps not
worthy of enshrinement in Nethack's eternal feature set, even if they
are cool to do.  Whether they deserve to be kept between versions, or
"fixed", I cannot say (and the devteam may very well think they are
worth keeping).

But I think I'm safe in saying that if a new feature comes along that
makes any of these things moot, that we shouldn't necessarily complain
simply because the game has changed.  We shouldn't hold on to old
aspects to the game if they must be removed to make possible something
more interesting, or more balanced, or more logical.  (Now, whether the
new features that displace the old ones are worth it, THAT is a matter
worth arguing over....)

